# SwimAI — Project Rules

## Stack
- Next.js 16 (App Router), React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, shadcn/ui
- Package manager: pnpm only. Never suggest npm/yarn commands or generate
 package-lock.json/yarn.lock.
- Icons: lucide-react only.
- CSS: Tailwind v4 with `@import 'tailwindcss'` + `@tailwindcss/postcss`
 PostCSS plugin. No tailwind.config.ts — design tokens live in `app/globals.css`.

## Content & i18n
- All user-visible copy lives in `messages/fr.json` (primary) and
 `messages/en.json` (secondary), accessed via next-intl's `useTranslations()`
 hook. Never hardcode user-visible strings directly in JSX.
- `lib/content.ts` is deprecated/unused — do not add new copy there, and flag
 it for removal if you encounter it.
- French is the primary/default locale. Every new string added to `fr.json`
 must have a matching key added to `en.json` in the same change.
- Never invent translations silently — if unsure of an English phrasing, flag
 it rather than guessing.

## Components
- One component = one file, PascalCase filename matching the default export.
- Functional components only, typed props via an explicit `interface` (not inline
 object types), e.g. `interface CoachCardProps { coach: Coach }`.
- No inline `style={{...}}` objects except for things Tailwind genuinely cannot
 express (e.g. dynamic background-image URLs, gradient stops with alpha values).
 Everything else must use Tailwind utility classes.
- No raw hex/oklch colors in component files. All colors must reference the
 design tokens defined in `app/globals.css` (e.g. `bg-teal-accent`,
 `text-text-secondary`, `fill-star-gold`).
 - This rule applies to `.tsx` component files and any other non-CSS files.
 - `app/globals.css` is the token source of truth and is expected to contain
 raw hex/oklch values in its `:root` and `.dark` variable blocks —
 that is correct, not a violation.
- Shared types (`Coach`, `NavLink`, etc.) live in `/types`, never
 re-declared inline in multiple components.

### Content-card design system (canonical glass-card recipe)
- ONE canonical surface treatment for content cards app-wide:

```
rounded-3xl border-blue-300/20 bg-blue-400/[8%] shadow-xl
shadow-black/20 backdrop-blur-md
```

- Applied to: coach cards, pricing cards, for-coaches benefit cards, footer
 strip, testimonial card, dashboard stat/booking/message/schedule cards,
 dashboard sidebar + content frame, onboarding shell containers, login/signup
 form containers, coach profile page panel, coaches-listing filter sidebar.
- Documented exceptions — do NOT apply this recipe to these, they are
 intentionally distinct:
  - Status-coded cards (`components/dashboard/coach-pending-requests.tsx`)
    — teal/amber/faded-white color-coding is semantic (accepted/pending/
    declined). Converting these would erase meaningful state signaling.
  - Dashed-border "add new item" affordances (e.g. coach schedule's
    "Add slot" button) — dashed border + transparent bg is a deliberate
    "click here to add" UX convention, distinct from content cards.
  - Signup `RoleCard` components (`app/[locale]/signup/page.tsx`) — hover
    state intentionally shifts teal to match the client/coach role icons
    inside; do not normalize to a single hover color.
  - Featured/headline callouts that need to stand out from sibling cards
    (the for-coaches "Inscription et 3 premières réservations" callout,
    How It Works' Step 3 card) — these keep a distinct teal-gradient
    identity (`border-teal-accent/40 bg-gradient-to-r from-teal-accent/15
    via-teal-accent/5 to-transparent`) precisely BECAUSE they need to
    visually outrank their neighbors. Don't "fix" this inconsistency —
    it's load-bearing hierarchy.
  - Floating popovers, the shared Dialog, and filter/selection pills —
    see their own existing sections below; these were never part of the
    content-card system to begin with.
- When adding a NEW content card anywhere in the app, use this recipe by
 default. Only deviate if the card needs one of the above documented
 exception reasons — and if so, document the new exception here too.

## Routing
- Every `<Link>` or button that implies navigation must point to a real route —
 never `href="#"` or an empty `onClick`. If the destination page doesn't exist
 yet, create a minimal stub page rather than leaving a dead link.
- Routes follow the locale-prefixed structure: `/[locale]/page-name`.
- **Documented exception:** `href="#"` on "Subscribe" (S'abonner) is accepted
 pending real payment infrastructure. "Become a coach" (Devenir coach) was
 removed from this exception list — `/signup/coach` and the full coach
 onboarding chain (`/onboarding/coach` → profile → availability → done) exist
 and work end-to-end; both "Devenir coach" CTAs now link there directly.
 Any OTHER new `href="#"` must be explicitly flagged in a comment.

### Buttons (canonical CTA system)
- ONE Button component: `components/ui/button.tsx`
- Three variants, all `rounded-xl` (NEVER `rounded-full` pills, NEVER ad-hoc
 inline button styling):
 - `variant="primary"` (teal) — "perform the action": search, book, confirm,
 save, submit, continue
 - `variant="entry"` (blue) — "enter / start the journey": Commencer, Dashboard,
 Become a coach, Subscribe
 - `variant="ghost"` (transparent) — tertiary actions
- The shape (`rounded-xl`) is shared across ALL variants. Color signals role,
 shape signals "this is a button." Never use `rounded-full` or pill-shaped
 buttons.
- Supports optional `icon` (left) and `iconRight` (right) props (lucide-react
 components). Supports `href` prop for polymorphic rendering via Next.js Link.
- Language switcher is a segmented control, NOT a Button — it's a setting
 indicator, not an action.

## Images
- All raster images go through `next/image` (`<Image />`) with explicit width,
 height, and alt text — never a bare `<img>` tag.
- Decorative/background images that must be CSS `background-image` (not `<Image />`)
 should be marked `aria-hidden="true"` and use the optimized WebP version of the
 asset (not PNG/JPG) — see `public/underwater-hero.webp` as the existing pattern.
- New image assets must be compressed/converted to WebP before being added to
 `/public`. Flag any asset over ~200 KB for review before committing.

## Accessibility
- Semantic HTML required: `<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<section>`, `<footer>`
 — not generic `<div>` soup for structural elements.
- All interactive elements must be keyboard-reachable and have visible focus
 states.
- Decorative-only elements (background images, icon-only flourishes) get
 `aria-hidden="true"`.

### Cursor and focus-visible states (systemic, not per-element)
- EVERY clickable element needs `cursor-pointer` explicitly — contrary to
 common assumption, native `<button>` elements do NOT get a pointer cursor
 by default (the CSS UA default is `cursor: default`). This must be set
 explicitly on every button, and on any non-button element with an `onClick`
 handler (e.g. a `motion.div` used as a dismiss backdrop).
- The canonical focus-visible treatment for native buttons (NOT the shared
 `Button`/`Pill`/`SelectTrigger` components, which have their own established
 rings):

 ```
 focus-visible:outline-none focus-visible:ring-2
 focus-visible:ring-teal-accent/60
 ```
- Disabled buttons intentionally KEEP their focus-visible ring — do not add a
 `disabled:focus-visible:ring-0` override. A focusable-but-disabled element
 should remain keyboard-discoverable (W3C guidance); only
 `pointer-events-none`/`opacity-40` communicate the disabled state, the
 focus ring stays.
- When adding any new native `<button>` or `motion.button`, include both
 `cursor-pointer` and the focus-visible ring from day one — don't rely on a
 later audit to catch it.

## Established Patterns

### Animations
- Framer Motion is used for the dashboard sidebar (motion.div width
 transitions, AnimatePresence for label fade/slide). Follow this existing
 pattern for any new animated UI — do not introduce a different animation
 approach (e.g. CSS keyframe animations for sidebar collapse/expand).

### Dialogs / Modals
- Use `components/ui/dialog.tsx`, which wraps Base UI's
 `Dialog.Root / Portal / Backdrop / Popup`. This is NOT Radix.
 Do not use a "Positioner" API — it does not exist in this setup.

### Auth
- `useAuth()` from `lib/auth/auth-context.tsx` is the single auth source.
 Any component that needs the logged-in user's data MUST be a `'use client'`
 component that reads `useAuth()` directly. Never hardcode a placeholder
 name or email "for now."

### next-intl API (important gotchas)
- `i18n/navigation.ts` exports `{ Link, redirect, useRouter, usePathname }`
 as named exports only — no default export. Always use `import { Link } from ...`
 (never `import Link from ...`).
- `useTranslations()` does not expose a `.locale` property. To get the current
 locale in a client component, call `useLocale()` from `next-intl` separately.
- In server components, use `getTranslations` from `next-intl/server` (async).
 In client components, use `useTranslations` from `next-intl` (sync).
- next-intl's router (from `i18n/navigation`) auto-prefixes the current
 locale on `push()` / `Link href`. ALWAYS pass UNPREFIXED paths
 (e.g. `/coaches/sophie-chen`, not `/fr/coaches/sophie-chen`). Mixing a
 prefixed path with next-intl's router produces double-locale URLs like
 `/fr/fr/coaches/sophie-chen`. If you need to navigate without locale
 handling, use `next/navigation`'s router instead — but that's almost
 always wrong in this app since pages are locale-prefixed.
- next-intl supports localized URL path segments via the `pathnames` config
 in `i18n/routing.ts` (e.g. `/coaches` → `/recherche` in FR, `/search` in EN).
 Once `pathnames` is configured, EVERY route not explicitly listed in the
 `pathnames` map fails strict type-checking on `Link`/`router.push`. If you
 add a new top-level route, add it to the `pathnames` map (even as
 `'/new-route': '/new-route'` for an unlocalized route) or every existing
 `Link`/`router` call elsewhere may start failing to type-check.
- Dynamic segments (e.g. `/coaches/[id]`) require the OBJECT form when
 navigating: `{ pathname: "/coaches/[id]", params: { id } }` — a plain string
 path is rejected once `pathnames` is configured.
- For locale-switching ON a dynamic route, pass BOTH `pathname` (from
 `usePathname()`) AND `params` (from `useParams()`) to `router.replace()` —
 omitting `params` loses the dynamic segment when switching locale.

### shadcn defaults in dark mode
- shadcn/ui components ship with `dark:` variant classes that have
 HIGHER specificity than plain Tailwind utility classes (e.g.
 `dark:bg-input/30` on `SelectTrigger`, dark-mode default backgrounds on
 popover surfaces, etc.).
- When overriding shadcn styling in dark-themed UI, plain `bg-transparent`
 will NOT override `dark:bg-input/30` — Tailwind's `dark:` variant
 always wins.
- Always add explicit `dark:` prefixed overrides:
 `dark:bg-transparent dark:hover:bg-transparent
 dark:data-[state=open]:bg-transparent`.
- If the library uses non-Tailwind CSS (e.g. react-day-picker's `rdp-*`
 classes) with their own dark mode rules, use `dark:!utility` (!important)
 to win over them.
- Base UI's `Select` and `Popover` share the same `modal` prop but have
 OPPOSITE defaults: `Select.Root`'s `modal` defaults to `true` (locks page
 scroll while open), `Popover.Root`'s defaults to `false` (page stays
 scrollable). If a `Select` dropdown unexpectedly locks page scroll while a
 `Popover` next to it doesn't, this is why — set `modal={false}` explicitly
 on the shared `components/ui/select.tsx` wrapper (already done) rather than
 passing it per call site.

### Floating popover design system
- The app has ONE canonical visual treatment for floating popover
 surfaces (dropdowns, calendar pickers, future tooltips, etc.):

 ```
 rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[8%]
 shadow-xl shadow-black/20 backdrop-blur-md text-white
 ```
- Hover/focus state for items inside these popovers:
 `focus:bg-white/10 focus:text-white`.
- Source of truth: `components/date-picker-field.tsx` popover,
 `components/search-bar.tsx` `SelectContent`.
- When adding new floating UI (any new popover, dropdown, or tooltip),
 match this exact treatment. Do NOT introduce a new visual style for
 floating surfaces — consistency across these elements is the design
 system contract.

### Customizing third-party components
- When a third-party component (react-day-picker, shadcn primitives,
 Base UI) doesn't behave as needed, prefer REPLACING the offending part
 with custom JSX over fighting the library's internal CSS.
- Example: react-day-picker v10's Nav was structurally hard to reposition
 via CSS. Fix: pass `hideNavigation={true}` and render a custom header
 with our own buttons. Took one round; CSS-only patches took six rounds
 and never fully worked.
- Sign you should switch from "patch the library" to "replace the part":
 if you've made 2+ CSS attempts and each produces a different failure
 mode, the architecture is the problem, not the styling.
- When a Base UI component renders its value/label display separately from
 its option list (e.g. `Select.Value` vs. `Select.Item`), do NOT assume the
 value display auto-resolves a label from the selected option. Base UI's
 `<Select.Value>` renders the RAW stored value by default; it only shows a
 translated/formatted label if you pass `children` as a function:
 `<Select.Value>{(value) => translateLabel(value)}</Select.Value>`. Passing
 `undefined` as children does NOT trigger a placeholder fallback once a real
 value is set — Base UI considers any non-null value "selected" and skips
 the placeholder branch entirely.

## General
- Before adding a new dependency, check if shadcn/ui, lucide-react, or an
 existing util already covers the need.
- Run `pnpm build` locally before considering a feature "done" — it must build
 with zero errors.

## Spacing/layout disputes — measure, don't calculate
- When a visual spacing/gap issue persists across 2+ rounds of `className`
 value changes that don't match the rendered result, STOP recalculating from
 source code. Source-level math (padding + margin + line-height + parent
 overflow, etc.) frequently fails to predict real rendered spacing, especially
 with sticky/floating elements, custom line-heights, or multi-layer wrapper
 components.
- Instead: take a screenshot of the actual rendered page, measure the gap
 directly from the image (even approximately), and apply ONE direct value
 change based on that real measurement. This converges in 1-2 rounds;
 continued on-paper recalculation can drag on indefinitely without ever
 matching what's actually on screen.
